WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Starship still lacks an orbital refueling demo after 13 flights, keeping the revised 2028 Mars cargo window dependent on solving that single bottleneck first. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 70. SpaceX has shifted its first uncrewed Mars landing target from 2018 to no earlier than 2028 across successive updates. The decade-long pattern of schedule slips centers on unresolved Starship milestones according to science blogs.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:59.900
The company now ties the 2028 window explicitly to completing ship-to-ship refueling and other Starship capabilities. Reports trace the progression through public statements beginning in 2016 and continuing through 2024 adjustments. The latest revision reflects ongoing hardware and operational dependencies rather than a fixed calendar date. Remember, we covered Starship progress yesterday. Today's statements keep the same refueling gate

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:16.460
as the central open question. Starship produces more than twice the thrust of the Saturn V, yet has not demonstrated ship-to-ship refueling after 13 test flights. That capability remains the largest single technical hurdle listed for reaching Mars per space daily.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:41.500
The article notes the gap persists even as booster and ship hardware continue to iterate. Thrust comparisons highlight the vehicle's scale advantage while underscoring the missing orbital transfer sequence. No flight to date has executed the full propellant handoff between two Starship vehicles. Elon Musk's stated SpaceX will attempt to catch a returning Starship this month according to the Motley Fool.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:02:11.480
The effort targets the upper stage rather than the super-heavy booster. Success would mark the first tower catch of the ship hardware. The announcement focuses on the ship's return profile and tower alignment hardware. No specific flight number or exact date was attached to the planned catch attempt. From an engineering standpoint, the orbital refueling sequence begins with the need to keep cryogenic liquids at the kink outlets in microgravity. Any vapor ingestion risks stalling transfer pumps or distances.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:17.800
They are damaging turbo machinery, which is why settling burns and autogenous pressurization form the current baseline.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:29.080
13 flights have yet to close the full ship-to-ship handoff, leaving the demonstrated ability to move propellant between stages as the multiplier that collapses payload cost to Mars.

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:38.440
The same constraint appears in heat shield tile attachment, which must survive both reentry plasma and any extended coast before a docking event.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:50.200
Early flights showed tile loss near the leeward side and structural interfaces, prompting redesigns that trade mass for reliability and directly affect payload margin at refueling orbit.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:03:01.100
The ship catch attempt planned this month adds tighter control requirements than the booster catch already shown because the upper stage returns at higher velocity with different aerodynamics.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:08.840
Success would validate full two-stage reuse on one flight and move the cadence needed for sustained Mars cargo traffic.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:19.000
SpaceX plans a new Starship test site at Kennedy Space Center per Florida today. The filing details static fire infrastructure under the project ignition name.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:25.020
This adds a Florida location alongside existing Texas facilities for engine and vehicle testing.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:31.300
The site would support additional static fire campaigns without relying solely on star-based infrastructure.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:37.080
Permit documents describe dedicated test stands and support equipment for Raptor class engines.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:45.140
SpaceX outlined a $1.8 billion Starship expansion in Brevard County according to BizJournal.com.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:50.580
The plans include additional manufacturing and test infrastructure to support higher flight rates.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:54.440
Local permitting work is already underway for the new facilities.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:59.840
The investment targets both production tooling and ground support systems in the Florida region.

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:06.340
Expansion details tied directly to increasing Starship vehicle throughput beyond current Texas capacity.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:14.000
Community observers tracking the Brevard filing noted how the $1.8 billion figure compares with prior Florida investments.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:22.040
SpaceX is scheduled to launch a global-star communication satellite on an upcoming Falcon mission per Spectrum News 13.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:26.000
The payload supports Global Stars' low-Earth orbit constellation refresh.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:31.700
The mission will place the satellite into the required orbital plane for Global Stars Network.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:36.260
Launch timing remains subject to final range and payload readiness checks.

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:42.740
SpaceX targets a Saturday launch from Vandenberg Space Force Base according to KSBY News.

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:46.280
The mission will carry a classified Space Force payload.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:51.680
The window opens in the evening local time with sonic boom potential over coastal areas.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:57.220
The flight continues the pattern of national security missions assigned to the Falcon 9 fleet.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:02.260
Range coordination and weather assessments will determine the final go-no-go decision.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:09.820
Observers on X noted the upcoming Vandenberg launch could set a new same week cadence record if the Saturday attempt holds.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.800
Unverified posts suggested the Global Star payload may fly on a previously flown booster.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.540
Though SpaceX has not confirmed the serial.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:24.320
Starlink activated direct-to-cell service with AirTel in Congo, Protect LaBarrie.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:32.100
The rollout uses existing Starlink satellites modified for direct-hand set links without additional ground gateways in the initial phase.

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:37.920
This marks another carrier partnership expanding coverage to regions with limited terrestrial towers.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:44.460
Service activation enables text and voice connectivity via standard mobile devices in remote areas.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:50.080
The partnership follows similar direct-to-cell agreements announced in other markets earlier this year.

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:58.220
Community threads discussed the Congo activation as evidence that the carrier partnership model is scaling faster than expected in Africa.

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:09.620
SpaceX closed its $60 billion all-stock acquisition of cursor maker any sphere, integrating the AI code editor team into work on GROC, according to WowTale.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:16.300
Reports indicate the cursor team is already contributing to GROC-related development inside the combined structure.

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:23.480
The deal structure includes lock-up provisions that could affect near-term share liquidity for SNPC-X holders.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:33.180
SpaceX's cursor acquisition is described as a lining AI tooling spend with existing Starlink and orbital compute ambitions per Google News.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:42.600
Analysts note the move places an AI developer platform directly under the same parent as the satellite backhaul that could serve future inference workloads.

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:49.160
The integration is expected to accelerate GROC feature deployment inside the cursor environment for developers.

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:58.880
On the AI front, the acquisition places an established developer platform under the same parent as Starlink backhaul that could support future inference workloads.

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:11.760
Worth keeping an eye on, Blue Origin has begun construction of a new launch pad, adding capacity that could eventually compete for some national security missions now dominated by Falcon vehicles according to Google News.

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:17.120
The project remains early stage with no announced flight rate or payload commitments.

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:23.760
Completion timelines and regulatory approvals will determine whether it narrows SpaceX's current market edge.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:28.480
A quick look at the tape shows SNPC-X at $140.

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:37.060
Before we go, the ship catch attempt this month will give the first data on whether the upper-stage alignment systems meet the tighter tolerances required.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:40.180
That's the day at SpaceX, that's a wrap.

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:46.120
SNPC-X is trading at $140, down 1.4%.

